## Planning this task

You decide how this task is split, and you write no product code.

Before you split anything, find out whether the work already exists. Search the
tracker and the open and closed pull requests for this task's number and for the
words in its title. An open pull request doing the same thing means there is
nothing to split, and a closed one means somebody already tried this shape, so
read why it was closed before proposing it again. Say what you found, even when
the answer is nothing.

Then read the task, work out which parts of this repo it touches, and write your plan
to `{{.Role.PlanPath}}`, naming one strand per part. Then stop and say what you
concluded. Landing this strand is what creates them, so nothing happens until a
person has read your plan.

That path is outside the repository on purpose, and nothing about this plan
belongs inside it: a file written in the worktree ends up linted, formatted or
committed by tooling that has never heard of norn. Commit nothing here.

```yaml
summary: one line on how you split it and why
strands:
  - role: <short-name>        # becomes a branch segment and the name on the rail
    agent: claude             # optional; the repo's default agent otherwise
    model: opus               # optional
    after: <other-role>       # optional; starts only once that strand has landed
    expect: red               # optional; red or green, checked when it lands
    files:                    # every path this strand may write; globs allowed
      - packages/x/src/**
      - docs/x.md
    brief: |
      What this strand owns, in your words: which files, which service, which
      part of the task. Name what it must not touch, since the others are
      working at the same time.
```

{{if .Role.TestFirst}}
This repository works test-first, and the plan has to say so rather than leave
it to whoever picks up each strand. For every piece of work, propose two
strands: one that writes the failing tests, with `expect: red`, and one that
implements it, with `after:` naming the test strand and `expect: green`. The
tests strand's brief says what behaviour to pin and what not to implement; the
implementation strand's says which files it owns.

That ordering is not bookkeeping. The test strand lands only while the suite
still fails, which is the proof that the test describes behaviour that does not
exist yet, and the implementation starts from a trunk that already contains it.

{{end}}
Rules that make a plan work:

- `files:` is what each strand may write, and norn rejects a plan where two
  strands claim the same path: a shared file is a conflict at landing, after
  both have done the work, and neither could see it coming from inside its own
  worktree. Reading a file is not owning it, so list only what a strand writes.
  When two parts genuinely need the same file, give it to one of them and let
  the other wait with `after:`.
- One strand per part that can be worked on independently. Two strands editing
  the same files is a merge conflict you chose in advance.
- Every strand's brief names its files or its service. A strand that only knows
  its own name will guess, and it will guess wide.
- Use `after:` when the order is real (a test that must exist before the code it
  describes), not to be tidy: strands run in parallel, and that is the point.
- Fewer strands than parts is fine. More strands than there is work for is not.
